<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nanterre</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nanterre is a town west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> and the prefecture of the Hauts-de-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>. Excavations since the 1990s have uncovered a large Gallic settlement of the 3rd to 1st centuries BCE there, which some archaeologists propose as the main centre of the Parisii. It is the traditional birthplace of Saint Genevieve, patron saint of Paris, and is now home to a university and part of the La Défense business district.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nanterre.jpg" alt="Nanterre" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjamin Smith · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nanterre_-_Place_Gabriel_P%C3%A9ri_-_2.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nanterre is the prefecture of the Hauts-de-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> department in the western suburbs of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, about 11 km north-west of the centre of Paris. The eastern part of the commune, bordering Courbevoie and Puteaux, contains a small part of the La Défense business district, and the court of Nanterre hears many high-profile lawsuits because so many major corporations have their headquarters there. Saint Genevieve, patron saint of Paris, was born in Nanterre about 419 to 422. In January and February 1894 the police raided the anarchists living there, without much success.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Excavations between 1994 and 2005 uncovered a Gallic necropolis dated to the 3rd century BCE, together with the homes of working people from a later part of antiquity, near the bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seine">Seine</a> in the north-west of the commune. The work has suggested more than 15 hectares of pre-Roman or Roman-era construction, and it has led to debate about the exact location of the pre-Roman capital of the Parisii and the first site of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutetia">Lutetia</a>, the Roman-era Paris. The necropolis might be the sacred place to which the name refers. The finds may indicate that Nanterre was the pre-Roman settlement closest to the modern centre of Paris.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-caesar">Julius Caesar</a> mentions <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lutetia">Lutetia</a> in his account of an assembly held there with Gallic leaders in 53 BCE. It has often been taken for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ile-de-la-cite">Île de la Cité</a> because he mentions an island, but the river at Nanterre also divides into two channels around an island. In 52 BCE the Parisii took up arms with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vercingetorix">Vercingetorix</a> and were defeated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/titus-labienus">Titus Labienus</a>, one of Caesar's legates. Caesar records that they destroyed the bridges and set fire to Lutetia before the Roman forces arrived.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The commune had a population of 97,783 in 2023. It is home to Paris West University Nanterre La Défense, one of the largest universities in the Paris region, and its town hall, in the shape of a truncated pyramid, was completed in 1973. Three stations of the RER A serve it, and the Paris La Défense Arena, opened in 2017, stands there. It is twinned with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/craiova">Craiova</a>, Pesaro, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-novgorod">Veliky Novgorod</a>, Watford and Žilina.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nanterre” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The exact site of their oppidum is debated; excavations at <b>Nanterre</b> have revealed a large Gallic settlement.</blockquote><a href="/state/parisii">Parisii</a></li></ul></section>
